--- !u!114 &-7544514192700195878
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 456c7c2b477e4253b9e4faf9ed4b18a9, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.InstructionsParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Title:
    m_Untranslated: Instructions
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: '1. Open the <b>Statistics panel</b> again. Notice that your
      robot model has over 500k triangles. CAD models typically have very dense models
      that are not really optimised for real-time usage.


      2. In the <b>Hierarchy</b>,
      select the <b>Moto</b> parent GameObject (top-level model).


      3. From
      the <b>Unity Asset Transform Toolkit</b> toolbar, select <b>Decimate</b>.


      4.
      Set <b>Criterion</b> to <b>Quality</b>.


      5. Set <b>Mesh Quality Preset</b>
      to <b>Low</b>.


      6. Select <b>Run</b>.


      7. Look at the <b>Statistics
      panel</b> again. You''ll notice there is a 66% reduction in the triangles.'
  m_CriteriaCompletion: 0
  m_Criteria:
    m_Items: []
--- !u!114 &-7336543746748614697
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: When creating a CAD model, it is crucial to document every manufacturing
      and assembly detail. This often involves modeling features like openings for
      fasteners (screws, bolts, etc.), which adds geometry to the model. However,
      these detailed openings are frequently unnecessary in a real-time model, as
      parts can simply intersect in areas not visible to the user.
--- !u!114 &-7185470601950052055
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 456c7c2b477e4253b9e4faf9ed4b18a9, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.InstructionsParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Title:
    m_Untranslated: Instructions
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "1. In <b>Assets</b> > <b>3DModels</b>, locate the newly imported
      <b>Moto prefab</b> again and drag it into the <b>Hierarchy</b> like you did
      before. \n\n2. Reset the <b>Transform</b> again in the <b>Inspector window</b>
      to centre the prefab in the scene.\n\n3. Select the <b>Moto prefab</b> in the
      <b>Hierarchy</b>.\n\n4. Observe that each part consists of an empty parent
      <b>GameObject</b> with a child mesh <b>GameObject</b>. The parent contains
      the metadata component and the mesh is the actual geometry. You will now merge
      those two into one <b>GameObject</b>."
  m_CriteriaCompletion: 0
  m_Criteria:
    m_Items: []
--- !u!114 &-6982373709377329940
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
